A||||9||RiceCooker||Unbelievable Sound||||14||
Z||000000||RiceCooker||06-23-2000||06:56 PM||||I just started my sound project for the RiceCooker. When i got it last year i immediately put in a JVC head unit with cassette and a 12 disc changer along with JBL 4x10's and 4x6's. I now wish i would have put in 4"ers with tweeters. Anyways i took my speakers out of my old Chrysler dynasty (a.k.a. grannymobile, burgundy box, etc) last week and plan to put them in my fiero. I have sony 5 1/4"ers with 1 1/2 inch tweeters and 3way alpine 6x9's. I plan to hook up the new additions to a 200watt amp or so which will fit under my seat. The 6x9's are going in the doors as i have seen 8 inch subs installed here before. You might need an little craftsmanship here and you will lose the door pockets but you can reuse the door fabric so it doesn't look silly. One 5 1/4 is going in the center glove box inside a small custom box which i have already made. Then i will cut a hole in the front of the glove box and cover it with the same material that covers the 4x10 speakers up front so i can keep a factory look. Same with the other 5 1/4 that i will cut into the middle of the dash up front. The two tweeters will be cut into the console between the CD player and the shifter on each side of my security display. I can manage all of the above cutting and mounting mostly myself but i need advice on wiring, wattage, etc Cause i don't know much of anything in that department. By the way, down the road i may get subs behind the seats but this is good for now. When i had my alpine 6x9's in my other car, all i heard was: "What size are your subs?" That's how much they kick a**!"<BR>Anyways, sorry for the superlong post but i'm pretty excited. <BR>Any ideas, comments, questions, and advice is appreciated. Thanks ||||reg||14
Z||000001||Oreif||06-23-2000||07:16 PM||||Wouldn't you want the tweeters up in the dash near the edges of the door? This would give the high freq's good seperation. Low freqs are omni-directional which basically means you can have the woofers anywhere you want and still hear the sound. High freqs are directional so having both tweeters that close together in the middle of the car is going to hurt your stereo seperation. If the 6X9 is playing left channel sounds and the tweeter on the console is also playing left channel sounds, You will never hear the right channel. A basic rule of setting up a stereo system in a car is low freq speakers low, High freq speakers high. The higher and farther apart the tweeters are the better your stereo seperation gets and the better the music sounds. The rest of the system sounds great! I would just move the tweeters.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||RiceCooker||06-23-2000||07:29 PM||||Hey thanks Oreif, but slow down, I'm actually learning something. JJ<P>Would it work if I switched the tweeter (left on right side, right on left side) and faced them a little bit away from each other (towards the doors) Remember, i do have high frequencies also in my two way 4x10's up front, 4x6's behind, and 3 ways in the doors.<P>Could that work?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||06-23-2000||09:31 PM||||If you really want a great sounding car, Since you are putting the 5 1/4" in the middle (front and back), Why dont you get a center channel processor? Audio Control makes one. The model # is ESP-3. It is a surround sound processor with a center channel output. You can leave the tweeters where you want them. The sound that comes out of the center channel speakers is L - R. In other words it takes the left channel and subtracts the right channel. So the sounds that come out the center channel is a "not" compliment of each side. This would give the driver and the passenger their own "enviroment". All sound would seem to center on each person. It is basically how DTS works in a movie theater. So no matter where you sit, you have perfect stereo separation. I don't know what kind of music you listen to but if you play classical, Jazz, rock, pop, or even some easy going rock ballads it will sound as if you are in your own private concert hall. The ESP-3 also has a spatial filter that is adjustable. It adds delays between octives. You could set the delay so that it sounds like the band is playing outside the car doors and the singer is on the hood! I have the ESP-2 which does the same thing but doesn't have the center channel output. You would be amazed at how it sounds!||||reg||14
Z||000004||Oreif||06-23-2000||09:33 PM||||By the way does it show I installed and set-up car stereo systems for 10 years?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000005||Monkeyman||06-24-2000||09:13 AM||||Personally, I'd ditch the 6x9s in favor of some round speakers.  Ought to be better sound since sound waves are round not oval.  Just my $3.46 (inflation).||||reg||1
Z||000006||phillip||06-24-2000||09:39 AM||||i've heard the same as monkey on the 6X9's, had to do with uneven cone flex.<P>i went cheaper with my center speakers. found an old soundstream x-over that had a center channel output. my single rear is in a pod that is part of my 3rd brake light (4" f and r centers) gets mixed mono fron an in dash eq that has the rear outs mixed together with a dual to single rca. you'd think it would short out but i saw the idea in a mag. and it works.<P>besides trying to work on my sub system a bit (1 8", plan to switch to 3), i want to try using a guitar effects pedal to add delay to my rear center. saw that in a mag too but haven't found a cheap pedal to experiment with.||||reg||1
Z||000007||coyote||06-26-2000||03:00 PM||||If you already have the speakers in the doors and dash i would ditch the extra tweeters, maybe add a center channel instead. if you want to stage the car then put the tweeters in the rearview mirror. if you still want the extra seperation then dash mount the tweeters as far into the corners as possible, you want them far and wide. typiaclly mid bass to sub is omnidirectional about 250 HZ on down. center chanell i would run from 250 to 5000 hz that should pick up most vocal ranges. as far as round versus oval cones i am struggling with that now, while you get a better wave from round the ovals offer more surface area and theroretcally then you have more air displacement. Dont know wich way to go there.<BR>Good luck with the install<P>Coyote||||reg||1
Z||000008||Getaway2k||06-26-2000||05:54 PM||||Hi Everyone...I am looking to replace my entire stereo system in my GT. You guys are giving me some great ideas. Anyone have any pictures of completed/in-process projects? How about illustrations on how to do some of this **** ? I was disappointed to see only 2 brands of 4x10's that were available to me thru Crutchfield (Pioneer and Jensen), however, reading the 4's idea mentioned here....pure genius. How should/can I position the 4 inch and tweeter on each side (using the mentioned 4x10 template) of the dash? Keep in mind I have no experience in this area and I don't want to hack up the interior at all.<P>Thanks to all, and keep the ideas coming.||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||06-26-2000||09:03 PM||||What gave me the better results,was aiming the tweeters towards the windshield,reflecting the right side tweeter sound into the driver's right ear,left tweeter into the passenger's left ear.<BR>Had to bend the little sheetmetal bracket that I made a couple times to get it sounding right from the driver's and passenger's seats.Curved glass reflects sound well.<BR>The delay of the crossover(capacitors),along with the sound bouncing off the windshield gives a nice delay,creating a theatre effect.<BR>Pretty much the same result of the tweeters being added in the middle of the car,but no extra tweeters or electronics,and it's all concealed under the front grills.I've seen some economically priced three way,separates that'd work great for this(4",tweeters in front,larger speaker in the doors,amp up behind dash).||||reg||1
